3. PERMISSIONS — WHAT YOU MAY DO

Subject to full compliance with Section 5 (Restrictions) and Section 7
(Termination), You may, free of charge:

3.1 View and Study. Browse the Software in the official repository, download
    complete copies of it, and read, learn, study, research and analyze the
    source code locally.

3.2 Local Modification. Perform secondary development and modification of
    the Software in a local environment, provided that every modified or
    derivative build remains on Your own machine(s) or on a private intranet
    and is NOT exposed or made accessible to the Public Network.

3.3 Demonstration. Use the Software for classroom demonstrations, internal
    teaching demonstrations, and local demo purposes.

3.4 Public Commentary. Create and publicly publish videos, blog articles or
    other content that introduces or reviews the Software, and quote short
    excerpts of the source code therein for explanation purposes, provided
    that such content is attributed to the Association and, where
    practicable, links to the official repository.

3.5 Community Contribution. Submit Issues, bug reports, feature suggestions
    and Pull Requests to the official repository. The Association retains
    the sole and final authority to accept, merge, modify or reject any
    Pull Request and any contribution.

4. SCOPE CLARIFICATION (BOUNDARY)

4.1 The restrictions in Section 5 apply ONLY to public-facing acts —
    public deployment, public distribution and public dissemination.

4.2 Private, non-public modification and use of the Software on Your own
    machine(s) or on a private intranet that is never exposed to the Public
    Network is not restricted by Sections 5.1 and 5.2, but remains subject
    to Sections 5.3, 5.4 and 5.5.

5. RESTRICTIONS — WHAT YOU MAY NOT DO

Without prior Written Permission from the Association, You must NOT:

5.1 Public Deployment. Deploy, host or operate the Software or any
    Derivative Work on the Public Network as a publicly accessible website
    or service.

5.2 Public Redistribution. Distribute, publish or otherwise make available
    for download, to the Public Network, the Software or any Derivative
    Work, whether complete or modified, in source or compiled form.

5.3 Commercial Use. Use the Software, in whole or in part, for any
    commercial purpose of any kind. Any commercial use requires prior
    Written Permission from the Association.

5.4 Reputation and Identity. In any public content (including videos, blog
    articles and Derivative Works):
    (a) damage, defame or otherwise harm the reputation of the Association;
    (b) impersonate the Association, or present any Derivative Work as an
        official or authoritative release of the Association;
    (c) create, promote or exploit division or schism within the
        Association or its community; or
    (d) maliciously mock, parody or defame the Association.

5.5 Notices. Remove, alter or obscure any copyright, attribution or license
    notice contained in or displayed by the Software.

